<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Durrani Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Durrani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Durrani Empire was an Afghan state founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> in 1747 after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a>. It raided northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> repeatedly, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a> in 1761 and further weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. Its loss of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> opened the way for the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/durrani-empire.jpg" alt="Durrani Empire" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AfghanTsakhtan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Durrani_Kingdom.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ahmad Khan, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> lineage of the Abdali tribe, was the son of Muhammad Zaman Khan, who had served as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, and a commander under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>. After Nader Shah's death in June 1747 a loya jirga, or grand council, met near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> for nine days. The two chief contestants were Hajji Jamal Khan of the Mohammadzai and Ahmad Khan. A holy man, Mohammad Sabir Khan, spoke for Ahmad Khan, reputedly placed sheaves of wheat or barley in his turban and crowned him with the title Durr-i-Durran, Pearl of the Pearls, from which the tribe took the name Durrani.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ahmad Shah took Qalati Ghilji and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> from the Ghilji and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> from the Mughal governor Nasir Khan. In 1749 the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-bahadur">Ahmad Shah Bahadur</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> to save his capital, and Ahmad Shah then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a> from Shahrukh Afshar and subdued the peoples north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>. He sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1757. In 1762 his forces killed thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> in the Punjab, in what Sikhs call the Vaḍḍā Ghallūghārā, and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, yet the Sikhs rebuilt it and he never subdued them. He died east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> in 1772.</p><p>His son Timur Shah Durrani took the throne in November 1772 and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> the capital. Timur's sons fought one another: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaman-shah-durrani">Zaman Shah</a>, who succeeded in 1793, was blinded and replaced by Mahmud Shah in 1801, and Shuja Shah ruled from 1803 to 1809. On 7 June 1809 Shuja signed with the British East India Company the first Afghan pact with a European power. By 1818 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> rulers held little more than Kabul and the land within 160 kilometres of it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> was lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> at the Battle of Shopian in 1819, and Ayub Shah was deposed in 1823.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The army was based on cavalry armed with flintlocks, who made hit-and-run attacks. Its core was 10,000 mounted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulams</a>, or slave-soldiers, carrying blunderbusses; a third of them were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> who had served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>. The bulk of the army was irregular Afghan tribal cavalry armed with lance and broadsword, and infantry played a very small part. Ahmad Shah balanced tribal alliances and hostilities, a balance his successors upset when they tried to rule without the other Durrani chiefs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> under Dost Muhammad Khan later displaced the dynasty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, and the empire is regarded as the foundation of the modern Afghan state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Durrani Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ahmad Shah Durrani, formerly commander of Nader's Abdali Afghan contingent, was chosen as ruler at Kandahar within months of Nader's death and founded the <b>Durrani Empire</b> that became the basis of modern Afghanistan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Conflict with the <b>Durrani Empire</b> of Ahmad Shah Abdali over the Punjab and northern India ended in the catastrophic defeat at the Third Battle of Panipat in 1761, which inflicted heavy military and human losses and disrupted revenue systems.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Durrani</b> ruler Zaman Shah withdrew from the Punjab, and in July 1799 Ranjit Singh seized Lahore from its Bhangi chiefs with the support of his mother-in-law Sada Kaur of the Kanhaiya Misl.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Buria arose around 1760, when Sikh sardars seized lands between the Sutlej and the Yamuna as Mughal and <b>Durrani</b> authority collapsed in the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/buria-state">Buria State</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamir Singh, a Brar Jat chief, established himself at Faridkot around 1763, when Sikh power spread across the plains after the <b>Durrani</b> wars.</blockquote><a href="/state/faridkot-state">Faridkot State</a></li><li><blockquote>Despite the <b>Durrani</b> massacre of 1762, they took Lahore in 1765 and rebuilt the Harmandir Sahib.</blockquote><a href="/state/sikh-misls">Sikh Misls</a></li></ul></section>
